1
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:03,439
Teams are made up of individuals and

2
00:00:03,439 --> 00:00:06,515
individuals are driven
by different motivators.

3
00:00:06,515 --> 00:00:09,808
Whether that's serving their organization,
supporting their family or

4
00:00:09,808 --> 00:00:11,960
simply working on projects
they think are cool.

5
00:00:12,990 --> 00:00:16,235
You'll need to learn about
the people on your team,

6
00:00:16,235 --> 00:00:20,534
in order to best determine how to
motivate everyone at the same time.

7
00:00:20,534 --> 00:00:21,562
A few years ago,

8
00:00:21,562 --> 00:00:26,570
researchers here at Google set out to
identify the dynamics of effective teams.

9
00:00:26,570 --> 00:00:28,285
And through this research,

10
00:00:28,285 --> 00:00:32,720
they identified five factors that
have an impact on team effectiveness.

11
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:39,286
In order of importance, those five
factors are: psychological safety,

12
00:00:39,286 --> 00:00:44,850
dependability, structure and
clarity, meaning and impact.

13
00:00:44,850 --> 00:00:45,750
Let's break these down.

14
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:48,916
Let's start with psychological safety,

15
00:00:48,916 --> 00:00:52,000
which is the number one factor
that impacts team effectiveness.

16
00:00:53,170 --> 00:00:54,840
Here's how our researchers define it.

17
00:00:56,240 --> 00:00:59,724
Psychological safety refers to
an individual's perception,

18
00:00:59,724 --> 00:01:02,759
of the consequences of taking
an Interpersonal risks.

19
00:01:03,850 --> 00:01:07,982
In other words, they believe it's safe
to take risks within their team and

20
00:01:07,982 --> 00:01:13,340
they don't risk being labeled as ignorant,
incompetent, negative or disruptive.

21
00:01:13,340 --> 00:01:15,673
On teams with high psychological safety,

22
00:01:15,673 --> 00:01:19,563
teammates feel comfortable taking
risks around fellow team members,

23
00:01:19,563 --> 00:01:24,250
seeking differing opinions and resolving
interpersonal conflict when it comes up.

24
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:30,480
For example, on my team at Google,
we like to say be direct and kind.

25
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:34,741
We've worked really hard to build a team
culture, in which we can hold one another

26
00:01:34,741 --> 00:01:39,220
accountable while maintaining a shared
space that is safe, secure, and peaceful.

27
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:44,001
What I found is that,
when opportunities to take risks do arise,

28
00:01:44,001 --> 00:01:48,140
like pitching an out-of-the-box
idea to my directors, for example,

29
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:52,206
this culture of mutual respect, has
already laid the groundwork to get direct

30
00:01:52,206 --> 00:01:56,940
feedback without frustration or
worrying that I might embarrass myself.

31
00:01:56,940 --> 00:02:00,893
And that's been invaluable in maintaining
a high level of psychological safety for

32
00:02:00,893 --> 00:02:01,400
the team.

33
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:04,770
Next, we have dependability.

34
00:02:04,770 --> 00:02:06,890
Google's researchers explain it this way.

35
00:02:07,890 --> 00:02:12,130
On dependable teams, members are reliable
and complete their work on time.

36
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:17,042
Creating a dependable team requires
the combination of setting,

37
00:02:17,042 --> 00:02:20,250
negotiating and meeting expectations.

38
00:02:20,250 --> 00:02:24,280
Yes, your team needs to meet
the expectations set for them.

39
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:25,265
At the same time,

40
00:02:25,265 --> 00:02:29,740
you as a project manager need to establish
a two way relationship with your team.

41
00:02:30,790 --> 00:02:33,644
You have to be able to clearly
communicate expectations and

42
00:02:33,644 --> 00:02:37,250
ensure that the team feels comfortable
negotiating with you when needed.

43
00:02:38,310 --> 00:02:41,983
For example, it's likely that
a person on your team works on two or

44
00:02:41,983 --> 00:02:44,290
more projects with competing deadline.

45
00:02:45,770 --> 00:02:48,780
If they're afraid to share
their own constraints with you,

46
00:02:48,780 --> 00:02:52,070
then their work on both
projects might suffer.

47
00:02:52,070 --> 00:02:56,862
Alternatively, if they come to you with
their concerns and open understanding and

48
00:02:56,862 --> 00:03:00,500
negotiation around priorities
could help ease their burden.

49
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:03,910
Next, we have a structure and clarity.

50
00:03:04,950 --> 00:03:06,550
Here's how our researchers define it.

51
00:03:07,620 --> 00:03:11,683
Structure and clarity refers to
an individual's understanding of job

52
00:03:11,683 --> 00:03:15,339
expectations, knowledge of how
to meet those expectations and

53
00:03:15,339 --> 00:03:17,720
the consequences of their performance.

54
00:03:19,010 --> 00:03:24,590
Each team member has a clear sense of
their individual role, plans and goals.

55
00:03:24,590 --> 00:03:27,240
And they have a sense of how
their work affects the group.

56
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:31,694
You as the project manager,
can help foster a sense of structure and

57
00:03:31,694 --> 00:03:33,750
clarity on the team.

58
00:03:33,750 --> 00:03:38,453
For example, if a project structure in
tracking are sloppy, unorganized and

59
00:03:38,453 --> 00:03:43,156
incohesive, then the team's output is
likely to be sloppy, unorganized and

60
00:03:43,156 --> 00:03:44,024
incohesive.

61
00:03:44,024 --> 00:03:45,810
This can cause tension within the team.

62
00:03:46,880 --> 00:03:51,268
Alternatively, if you diligently engage
in project tracking, your team will have

63
00:03:51,268 --> 00:03:55,120
clarity, feel more united and
will be able to effectively collaborate.

64
00:03:56,750 --> 00:03:59,640
Meaning, also impacts team effectiveness.

65
00:03:59,640 --> 00:04:03,831
Google's researchers defined
meaning in this context, as finding

66
00:04:03,831 --> 00:04:08,910
a sense of purpose either in the work
itself or in the results of that work.

67
00:04:08,910 --> 00:04:12,845
For example, your teammates might
find meaning in supporting themselves

68
00:04:12,845 --> 00:04:15,467
financially, helping
the team reach its goals or

69
00:04:15,467 --> 00:04:19,380
wanting their products to reach
a new community of users.

70
00:04:19,380 --> 00:04:21,900
And finally, we have impact.

71
00:04:21,900 --> 00:04:26,262
Our researchers define impact as
the belief that the results of

72
00:04:26,262 --> 00:04:29,152
one's work matters and creates change.

73
00:04:29,152 --> 00:04:30,419
It can be challenging for

74
00:04:30,419 --> 00:04:34,830
people to notice how their work can
shift an entire ecosystem forward.

75
00:04:34,830 --> 00:04:38,957
Part of your role as the project manager,
is to help individual teammates

76
00:04:38,957 --> 00:04:43,400
identify how they drive impact both
within the team and beyond it.

77
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:47,640
Project tracking can be a helpful tool for
visualizing progress and impact.

78
00:04:48,860 --> 00:04:52,559
Meeting milestones for example,
demonstrates to the team how their

79
00:04:52,559 --> 00:04:56,640
individual tasks contribute
to the larger project goals.

80
00:04:56,640 --> 00:05:00,720
For example, my team focuses
on routing within Google Maps.

81
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:06,170
The big idea is to focus on helping people
to get to where they're going on time.

82
00:05:06,170 --> 00:05:10,630
So, everything we do should make that
experience incrementally better.

83
00:05:10,630 --> 00:05:12,830
And that's how we add impact.

84
00:05:12,830 --> 00:05:17,390
To recap, Google's researchers
identified psychological safety,

85
00:05:17,390 --> 00:05:20,886
dependability, structure and
clarity, meaning and

86
00:05:20,886 --> 00:05:26,220
impact as the top five factors that
have an impact on Team effectiveness.

87
00:05:26,220 --> 00:05:26,886
Coming up,

88
00:05:26,886 --> 00:05:31,090
we'll discuss how project managers
lead teams that reach project goals.
